# Build-context excludes.
#
# docker-compose.yml sets `context: .` for all three images, so anything not
# listed here is uploaded to the daemon on every build, and anything a
# Dockerfile COPYs from it can end up in a distributable layer.

node_modules
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/__pycache__
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache

# `uploads` on its own matched only a repo-root directory that does not exist.
# apps/api/uploads — 104 MB of real patient scans on a developer machine,
# including a 10 MB camera-original JPEG and a 9-page genetic report PDF — was
# therefore inside the context of `COPY apps/api ./apps/api`. The documented
# deploy clones and builds on the server, where that directory is absent, so
# nothing has shipped; but any image built on a laptop (a routine
# `docker compose up --build` during dev, or a future CI/registry push) would
# bake them into a layer, invisible at runtime because the api-uploads volume
# is mounted over the same path.
**/uploads
**/minio-data

# Local env files, whichever machine builds. apps/api/.env exists on developer
# machines and would otherwise ride in on `COPY apps/api ./apps/api`, putting
# that developer's JWT_SECRET and DATABASE_URL into non-revocable registry
# history. The .example files are documentation and stay — note the negation
# has to come after the two patterns above it to win.
**/.env
**/.env.*
!**/.env.example

# Database dumps. scripts/db-backup.sh defaults BACKUP_DIR to <repo>/backups,
# so a dump of the whole PHI database can appear in the working tree; it must
# never reach a build daemon or a layer.
**/backups
**/*.dump
**/*.sql.gz

# The 547 MB knowledge-base source corpus. No Dockerfile copies it —
# kb-service reads the ingested chunks out of Postgres — but leaving it in the
# context means shipping it to the daemon on every single build.
content/medical-kb/source

.git
.DS_Store
